(1.1 Why a graph instead of relational tables?) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Relational tables are excellent for transactional records with known joins. Syrka's core ) Tj
T*
(intelligence problem is different: capabilities emerge from multi-hop, temporal, evidence-backed) Tj
T*
( relationships among people, learning activities, artifacts, assessments, credentials, research,) Tj
T*
( projects, labour-market demand, occupations, jurisdictions, and recommendations. These ) Tj
T*
(relationships change over time and must remain explainable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A graph is required because:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Capability is relational. A capability is not a column on a user row. It is an assertion ) Tj
T*
(connecting a person, capability concept, evidence, context, confidence, provenance, ontology ) Tj
T*
(version, and validity window.) Tj
T*
(2. Evidence is multi-hop. A Job Passport claim may trace from passport -> capability observation) Tj
T*
( -> evidence -> assignment -> artifact -> rubric -> course outcome -> ontology term -> ) Tj
T*
(occupation requirement.) Tj
T*
(3. Recommendations are path-based. Learning pathways, job matches, mobility routes, and ) Tj
T*
(curriculum gaps are graph traversals.) Tj
T*
(4. Ontology is relational. Skills require knowledge, capabilities require skills, occupations ) Tj
T*
(require capabilities, credentials verify claims, and jurisdictions regulate mobility.) Tj
T*
(5. Temporal evolution matters. Syrka must represent historical capability, current confidence, ) Tj
T*
(decay, supersession, revocation, and future potential.) Tj
T*
(6. AI needs grounded context. Graph retrieval gives AI systems constrained, provenance-rich ) Tj
T*
(neighborhoods rather than unstructured guesses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1.2 Why capability is represented as relationships rather than columns) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A column such as sql_skill_score = 0.84 hides the most important facts: what evidence supports ) Tj
T*
(the claim, what ontology version defined the capability, which model/rubric inferred it, whether) Tj
